\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 Sarah had not planned on the throne. She had planned on a poncho, a set of stick-on sideburns and half a million strangers, and the plan had survived contact with the festival for slightly under a minute. An Elvis on gate duty had looked clean through the disguise with the infallibility of the truly devoted, dropped to one knee in the trampled grass, and the whole conspiracy died of reverence on the spot. Her regalia had been waiting in a garment bag behind the merch tents, pressed and ready, which told her precisely how well her rebellions were priced into the forecasts. The gown was velvet, oceanic, and made no argument whatsoever for concealment: at thirty-four weeks Little Clinton rode out in front of her like the figurehead of a flagship, and the wardrobe Elvi had cut the cloth to announce the fact to the back rows and the stream besides. Eloise didn\'92t know she was here. The sentence had history. She had packed it specially.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Overhead, moored to the tower by a cable as thick as her wrist, floated Colonel Tom Porker. He was a pig the size of a delivery van, upholstered in a pink that no living pig had ever committed, wearing a Stetson scaled for a much larger pig and smoking a cigar that actually smoked: a fog unit concealed in the snout puffed rings of it downwind at civic intervals. The design committee had worked from first principles \'97 a Tom Parker was someone who took everything and gave back a hat \'97 and had been firm on the one point that mattered: no jumpsuit, because a Tom Parker never wore the costume; he sold it. The Colonel got the hat, the cigar, and an expression of untroubled appetite, and each time the breeze swung him out over a new province of the lawn, that province rose and booed him with enormous affection \'97 Tax the Tom Parkers! \'97 and the Colonel rotated serenely on his tether, taking his percentage of the air.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Below, the crowd did what crowds do when the day is warm, the music is loud and the drinks are structurally cream. Capes turned in the light like a spill of sequins. Air Guitars went off across the lawn in ones and twos, each drop-D detonation raising its own small ovation. A punch-up flared stage-left, was scored by the nearest ten thousand spectators on execution and degree of difficulty, and subsided into hugging. Nobody down there was worshipping anything, except possibly the Colonel, and that was booing. It was, Sarah thought, exactly the crowd she had ordered: vast, happy, and sublimely indifferent to any one woman in a poncho. From up here she could admire it the way you admire a country you\'92ve just been deported from.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The throne to her right stayed empty all afternoon. The Elvi had dressed it anyway \'97 cape arranged across the seat, crown of half-melted bobbleheads set on the cushion at a devotional angle \'97 so that it managed to be less an empty chair than a small monument to one. The Teacher himself was in the green room, by every report enjoying the bands, the rider and the Elvisettes at a distance from the platform that had been chosen with care and was not about production. Sarah kept her eyes off the throne the way you keep your tongue off a cracked tooth. The last conversation those two chairs had hosted had run to three words, and the three words had covered everything.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The aunts, at least, were present and accounted for. Kathy had come as escape crew and stayed as medical officer, tie-dyed lab coat over festival glitter, triaging a tray of themed cocktails with the gravity of a woman defusing dessert. \'93Medicinal,\'94 she ruled, taking one. \'93For the aunt.\'94 Every half hour or so she drifted across, laid the back of her hand against Sarah\'92s cheek in a manner she clearly believed was covert, said \'93just admiring you, sugar plum,\'94 and drifted back with a pulse, a temperature and a blood-pressure estimate she thought nobody had noticed her taking. Sarah let her have it. It was the cheapest medical care in Ulyssia, and it came with endearments. Hayley had spent the first hour establishing the platform\'92s three exits and the second hour pretending she hadn\'92t, and accepted a Blue Hawaii the way she accepted most things, without visible enthusiasm and to the last drop. Somewhere in the middle of the platform\'92s traffic \'97 runners with clipboards, an Elvis renewing the bunting, the Colonel\'92s shadow sliding across everything like a slow blessing \'97 Kathy\'92s eyes found Hayley\'92s over the tray and stayed a half-second past logistics, and nobody remarked on it, least of all them.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Cocktail hour came and went to a nine-piece band with two drummers, and the sun got down among the fig trees and set the Bubble faintly opalescent, and the lawn lit itself with ten thousand phone screens and the odd contraband sparkler. Sarah drank juice, shifted her spine against velvet that had been upholstered for spectacle rather than lumbar outcomes, and let the noise come up through the scaffolding into her bones. It was very nearly the state of grace. It was the state of grace with a throne under it and a camera on it, which is a different state, adjacent but not interchangeable, and she was still negotiating the difference when the chair arrived.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>It came up the platform stairs ahead of its owner, borne by a young man whose own tailoring suggested a life spent adjacent to greatness: a white executive recliner, aggressively ergonomic under a pelt of winter ermine, known to the trade press, the tax authorities of nine jurisdictions and its manufacturer\'92s lawyers as the Ermine Muller. The young man set it down beside Sarah\'92s throne, squared it to the stage with a surveyor\'92s care, and withdrew. It sat there beside the throne with the serene wrongness of a bank vault at a picnic. Then Eloise came up the stairs.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>She was dressed simply, which was the expensive way. The ensemble had no sequins, no cape and no visible label, and it had nevertheless cost more than the entire festival had \'97 a comparison the festival made easy, and one that held up even priced per gram against the Air Guitars. She crossed the platform without hurry, acknowledged Kathy with a nod and Hayley with a fractionally different nod, and did not look at Sarah at all, which was how Sarah knew she was the entire agenda. There was no reproach. There was no remark. There was a woman who had come down out of the Blue Mountains to a rock festival with her own office chair, and the fact of being found was permitted to do all of its own talking.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You\'92ll want the aisle seat, in your condition,\'94 Eloise said, and sat \'97 and Sarah understood that the chair had been squared to the stage on the inside deliberately, leaving Sarah the throne nearest the stairs, the shortest route, the practical flank.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Something happened in Sarah\'92s chest then, small and specific. Relief would have been ridiculous. Gratitude would have been worse. She declined to name it, the way she declined most incoming panic, on the long-tested grounds that these things generally arrive smaller than advertised \'97 though this one, she noted, was arriving at exactly the size advertised, which was new. Onstage the brass hit something enormous, and the baby rolled with it, a slow oceanic turn, and her hand was at her belly before she had been consulted. She retrieved it. Eloise, watching the stage, had seen everything and moved nothing. Sarah kept her face where she had left it and looked back out at the crowd \'97 the half million strangers she had come here to vanish into, going off like happy artillery in the dusk, every one of them anonymous except her. The view was unchanged. The escape was over. She stayed in it anyway.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>For a long while neither of them said anything, and the nothing was companionable in the way a ceasefire is companionable. Then Eloise tipped her chin at the tree line, where a row of her tanks stood along the perimeter with their guns politely elevated, hung with leis by persons unknown.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The hardware is on loan,\'94 she said, to the view. \'93Somebody has to supervise the depreciation.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93They look very happy,\'94 Sarah said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise had a tablet in her lap with the telemetry open, and in two hours she did not consult it once.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The light went down the rest of the way, and the day\'92s arithmetic reached its answer. The band onstage had grown all afternoon by the pyramid\'92s patient law \'97 strings now, a second keyboard, a choir riser filling with sequins \'97 until it finished its set at full quorum and did not leave. The roadies moved through it instead, laying cable, setting a single microphone at the centre mark with the ceremony of men positioning a crown. The screens, which had captioned everything all day, went dark. Half a million conversations leaned forward at once, and the sound of it came up the scaffolding like weather. On the perimeter the tanks sat garlanded; overhead the Colonel puffed his rings at the first stars through the Bubble; to Sarah\'92s right, the empty throne held its cape and its crown and its three old words. Eloise settled the ermine under her forearms in the manner of a woman intending to stay for whatever came.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Below them, the dark at the side of the stage rearranged itself, and half a million people waited for the High Elvis to walk out.\
